  "narration": [
    {
      "id": "oklo-v4-r2-cue-1",
      "transcript": "About two billion years ago, Earth had nuclear reactors. Nobody built them.",
      "start": 0,
      "duration": 5.35,
      "media": {
        "id": "media.cc2af928f822154fb753f2e21e36bdf8042e92dbf3b886419c9315064a98c591",
        "sha256": "cc2af928f822154fb753f2e21e36bdf8042e92dbf3b886419c9315064a98c591"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "oklo-v4-r2-cue-2",
      "transcript": "In 1972, uranium from Gabon held less uranium-235 than expected.",
      "start": 5.666666666666667,
      "duration": 7.425,
      "media": {
        "id": "media.12e33b8cb1bd77f17b5594ac17f7831562e037fb7d8b68edd684ca1c7c5e3c23",
        "sha256": "12e33b8cb1bd77f17b5594ac17f7831562e037fb7d8b68edd684ca1c7c5e3c23"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "oklo-v4-r2-cue-3",
      "transcript": "Fission fingerprints in the rock revealed ancient nuclear reactions. How could geology sustain them?",
      "start": 13.266666666666667,
      "duration": 6.95,
      "media": {
        "id": "media.c9284537b114c8a3a5a6634375dca7d8de3fb219780b69c37bc550055032c5c2",
        "sha256": "c9284537b114c8a3a5a6634375dca7d8de3fb219780b69c37bc550055032c5c2"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "oklo-v4-r2-cue-4",
      "transcript": "Back then, uranium had a higher share of that easily split isotope. Geology concentrated it into rich deposits.",
      "start": 20.533333333333335,
      "duration": 8.325,
      "media": {
        "id": "media.2738f38a00282264d4172d719a227cde91afeb42b6d66be7790cd7815281cacf",
        "sha256": "2738f38a00282264d4172d719a227cde91afeb42b6d66be7790cd7815281cacf"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "oklo-v4-r2-cue-5",
      "transcript": "Splitting uranium released neutrons. Collisions with hydrogen in groundwater slowed them, helping them split more uranium.",
      "start": 29.033333333333335,
      "duration": 8.475,
      "media": {
        "id": "media.c6caed83cb5b34ce7cb1d74fc216284498074b641844fbd27571d12e26fc0a27",
        "sha256": "c6caed83cb5b34ce7cb1d74fc216284498074b641844fbd27571d12e26fc0a27"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "oklo-v4-r2-cue-6",
      "transcript": "Enough avoided escape or absorption elsewhere to keep the chain going.",
      "start": 37.7,
      "duration": 4.825,
      "media": {
        "id": "media.eda92352d6be9c1f89c9c554439012b9c08e54c20396a1f8862b0c6a87a1d7c1",
        "sha256": "eda92352d6be9c1f89c9c554439012b9c08e54c20396a1f8862b0c6a87a1d7c1"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "oklo-v4-r2-cue-7",
      "transcript": "From one studied zone's isotopes, researchers infer starts and stops: heating drove water away; cooling let it return.",
      "start": 42.7,
      "duration": 8.4,
      "media": {
        "id": "media.74e5862ec9b16604f11eabfed4a361e7a1f29a28eddbc7564e88b4894a828cd4",
        "sha256": "74e5862ec9b16604f11eabfed4a361e7a1f29a28eddbc7564e88b4894a828cd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "oklo-v4-r2-cue-8",
      "transcript": "Reaction zones in rock. Heat, not electricity. Earth had assembled the conditions for fission, without machinery.",
      "start": 51.4,
      "duration": 8.1,
      "media": {
        "id": "media.59a13583d6eac708c7e2475387b9ae2d75856587a455be3e93498bec968fdd73",
        "sha256": "59a13583d6eac708c7e2475387b9ae2d75856587a455be3e93498bec968fdd73"
      }
    }
  ],
